// expect-error: NITPICK-LEX-004
// expect-error: NITPICK-PARSE-002
// expect-error-at: 63:18
//
// PROBE 02d — `int64`'s MINIMUM CANNOT BE SPELLED AS A LITERAL, in any width.
//
// THE THIRD TWIN, and the one that was not planned. It exists because writing
// SPAN_MODEL.md N-20b's range check sent somebody to VERIFICATION.md P-5's
// code sample, which said:
//
//     prove(total >= -9223372036854775808i128 && total <= 9223372036854775807i128);
//
// and that line DOES NOT COMPILE. The specification was teaching a shape the
// compiler refuses, in the one document whose whole subject is the checks this
// library's correctness rests on.
//
// ASKS: is `9223372036854775808` -- the magnitude of `int64`'s minimum --
// spellable as a literal when the destination is `int128`, which has room for
// it four times over?
//
// MEASURED, against compiler commit 950bb1d: NO. The envelope is a property of
// the LITERAL and not of its destination type:
//
//   NITPICK-LEX-004 …:63:18: this literal is outside the 64-bit literal
//   envelope (D-148); a type's outermost values are constructed
//   arithmetically, not spelled
//   NITPICK-PARSE-002 …:63:18: expected an expression
//
// Two codes, because the lexer having refused the token leaves the parser with
// no expression where one was required. D-237's rule -- the set of codes
// reported must EQUAL the set expected -- makes that worth stating: a harness
// entry for this file expects BOTH.
//
// AND UNTIL 0.0.2 THE HEADER NAMED ONLY ONE OF THEM. The prose above said
// "expects BOTH" and the machine-readable header carried a single
// `expect-error:`, so the file that exists to state D-237's rule was the file
// that broke it. The harness's first run reported `NITPICK-PARSE-002` as
// "reported and not expected", which is the rule working. The second marker
// was added then. A POSITION MARKER CITES A LINE IN ITS OWN FILE, so the
// second marker and this paragraph together moved the refusal down the file
// and `expect-error-at` had to move with it. The check catches that; the
// reason is written here so the next person to add a marker to a file that
// carries a position expects the move rather than debugging it.
//
// THE ASYMMETRY IS THE POINT. `9223372036854775807i128` (the MAXIMUM) is fine,
// because it is inside the envelope; only the minimum's magnitude is one too
// large. So a range check written by symmetry from a working upper bound is
// exactly the code that stops compiling, and the fix is arithmetic:
//
//     int128:LO = (0i128 - 9223372036854775807i128) - 1i128;
//
// which `tests/probe/probe02_int128.npk` verifies is the right value, at width,
// in both signs. Its `int64` form -- `0i64 - I64_MAX - 1i64` -- is the same
// idiom and is why probe 02 was written that way rather than by accident.
//
// DEPENDS ON THE ANSWER: VERIFICATION.md P-5, corrected in the same commit as
// this file (TM-105). Every range check that N-20b makes mandatory tests
// against int64's bounds, so every one of them needs the lower bound, and
// spelling it is the first thing anybody tries.
mod:probe02d_wide_literal_refused;

func:main = int32(cstring[]:_~argv) {
    int128:lo = -9223372036854775808i128;
    if (lo >= 0i128) { exit 10i32; }
    exit 0i32;
};

func:failsafe = int32(Error:e) {
    pick (e) {
        (HeapBadRequest) { exit 91i32; },
        (HeapOom)        { exit 92i32; },
        (IntOverflow)    { exit 93i32; },
        (OutOfBounds)    { exit 94i32; },
        (Unreachable)    { exit 95i32; },
        (WildLeak)       { exit 96i32; },
        (*)              { exit 99i32; }
    }
    exit 9i32;
};
